test(lifecycle): pin the expiry, horizon and drift defects (#254)

Three tests, written to fail, each naming one verified defect in the run
lifecycle. No production file is touched; RM-152 (#255) and RM-155 (#258)
are what turn these green.

SPI-008 (internal/spire/lifecycle_test.go) drives a run through lapse,
restore, a released credential and a second lapse against a real SPIRE and
a real chain. The reaper's idempotency key is the run id alone, so the
first lapse's event answers for every lapse afterwards while reap() deletes
regardless: the second sweep reports Recorded=false Deleted=true and the
ledger holds one run_expired where two lapses happened — an action with no
record (I3).

MCP-078 (internal/rundir/horizon_test.go) drives the shipped run directory
underneath the shipped get_credential. A run whose first lapse is 39 days
old, which worked five weeks later and lapsed again an hour before the
call, is refused restore under a 30-day horizon: the horizon is measured
from the earliest run_expired rather than from the lapse being restored
from. The restorer runs zero times.

REC-017 (internal/spire/lifecycle_test.go) reconciles a run whose newest
recorded fact is later than its run_expired and whose entry the restore
path re-created. The reconciler reports spire_entry_not_deleted and appends
a ledger_drift_detected: the legitimate repair is recorded as tampering,
and an alert is permanent (I4).

Doc 07 carries the three rows, and a note that SPI-008 is claimed twice —
RM-019 (#27) reserved it for SPIRE entry reconciliation in a row that was
never added to the catalogue. A human settles the numbering; both sets of
tests exist either way.

